<section class="page-hero">
  <div id="ambient-canvas" class="ambient-canvas"></div>
  <div class="container page-hero-inner">
    <span class="eyebrow">Free Tool</span>
    <h1>Free Sitemap Validator — Check a sitemap.xml for Errors</h1>
    <p>Paste your <strong>sitemap.xml</strong> and this <strong>free sitemap validator</strong> checks it against the rules Google actually enforces — valid XML, absolute URLs, the 50,000 URL / 50MB limits, and more. No signup, nothing leaves your browser.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="container contact-grid llms-tool-grid">
    <div>
      <form class="contact-form llms-form" id="sv-form" onsubmit="return false;" aria-label="Sitemap validator form">
        <div>
          <label for="sv-input">Paste your sitemap.xml contents</label>
          <textarea id="sv-input" style="min-height: 280px;" placeholder="<?xml version=&quot;1.0&quot; encoding=&quot;UTF-8&quot;?>&#10;<urlset xmlns=&quot;http://www.sitemaps.org/schemas/sitemap/0.9&quot;>&#10;  <url>&#10;    <loc>https://example.com/</loc>&#10;  </url>&#10;</urlset>"></textarea>
        </div>
      </form>
    </div>

    <div class="contact-info-card llms-preview-card">
      <div class="llms-preview-head">
        <strong>Validation results</strong>
        <span id="sv-status" class="llms-status">Waiting for input</span>
      </div>
      <pre id="sv-output" class="llms-output" style="max-height:320px;">Paste a sitemap to check it.</pre>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="container llms-explainer">

    <h2>What This Checks</h2>
    <ul class="service-points">
      <li><strong>Valid XML</strong> — the file must parse without errors; one malformed tag can make crawlers discard the whole file</li>
      <li><strong>Correct root element</strong> — a <code>&lt;urlset&gt;</code> (or <code>&lt;sitemapindex&gt;</code> for a sitemap of sitemaps) in the sitemaps.org namespace</li>
      <li><strong>Every <code>&lt;url&gt;</code> has a <code>&lt;loc&gt;</code></strong> — the one required child element</li>
      <li><strong>Absolute URLs</strong> — relative paths in <code>&lt;loc&gt;</code> are invalid; every URL needs a full scheme and host</li>
      <li><strong>One consistent host</strong> — a sitemap should only list URLs from the domain (or subdomain) it's served from</li>
      <li><strong>No duplicate URLs</strong> — the same <code>&lt;loc&gt;</code> listed twice wastes crawl budget and can confuse priority signals</li>
      <li><strong>Size limits</strong> — a maximum of 50,000 URLs and 50MB uncompressed per sitemap file, per Google's sitemap protocol</li>
      <li><strong>Valid <code>&lt;lastmod&gt;</code> dates</strong> — when present, it should be a real W3C Datetime value, not a placeholder</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Why a Sitemap Fails Silently</h2>
    <p>Google doesn't usually reject an invalid sitemap outright — it logs the error in Search Console's Sitemaps report and skips the broken entries, which means a typo or a relative URL can sit unnoticed for weeks while those pages simply never get crawled through that path. Running the raw file through a check like this before submitting it catches the structural problems a quick visual scan misses.</p>

    <h2>Sitemap Validator FAQs</h2>
    <div class="faq-list" style="margin-top: 22px;">
      <details>
        <summary>Does a sitemap need a &lt;lastmod&gt; for every URL?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>No, it's optional. When included, it should reflect a genuine last-modified date — Google's own guidance says it ignores lastmod values it doesn't trust, such as ones that update on every crawl regardless of real changes.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Can a sitemap mix URLs from different subdomains?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>Only if the sitemap is hosted at a location that covers all of them (for example, served from the root domain with the right path setup) — by default, a sitemap can only include URLs that share its own host.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What happens if I go over 50,000 URLs?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>Split the URLs across multiple sitemap files and list them all in a single sitemap index file — this tool flags the limit so you know when it's time to split.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Is a sitemap index file the same as a regular sitemap?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>No — a sitemap index uses <code>&lt;sitemapindex&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;sitemap&gt;</code> elements pointing to other sitemap files, rather than <code>&lt;urlset&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;url&gt;</code> elements pointing to pages. This tool detects which type you've pasted.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Does this tool store or send my data anywhere?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>No. Everything is checked in your browser with JavaScript — nothing is uploaded, logged, or stored on any server, including ours.</p>
      </details>
    </div>

    <div class="cta-band" style="margin-top: 44px;">
      <span class="cta-orb cta-orb-1"></span>
      <span class="cta-orb cta-orb-2"></span>
      <h2>Need your sitemap fixed, not just flagged?</h2>
      <p>Contomatix handles technical SEO cleanup end to end, not just diagnostics.</p>
      <div class="cta-actions">
        <a href="/contact" class="btn btn-primary">Get in touch</a>
        <a href="https://wa.me/<%= site.whatsappNumber %>" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="btn btn-glass">Chat on WhatsApp</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<script type="application/ld+json">
  <%- JSON.stringify({
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'WebApplication',
    name: 'Free Sitemap Validator',
